window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-10-20T04:55:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-10-19","event":"Tertiary industry index (Aug); Tokyo reaction to Friday's US tech selloff and $110 Brent","why":"Done: tertiary industry index −0.1% m/m (consensus −0.4%, prior +0.2%). Nikkei closed down 0.62% at 64,916.70 after a 2% intraday drop, TOPIX flat at 4,067.48; the Neutral test is still half met. Ueda told parliament underlying inflation is 'quite close' to 2%; Takaichi would not rule out bonds for the tax cut"},{"date":"2026-10-20","event":"20-year JGB auction; US AI earnings week begins","why":"Done: ¥700bn 20-year cleared at an average 3.723% (prior 3.856%) with a record 5.84 bid-to-cover (prior 4.01, 12-month average 3.73) and a 0.07 tail (prior 0.15); the 20-year yield fell sharply after the result"},{"date":"2026-10-21","event":"Trade balance and exports (Sep)","why":"Exports prior +19.3%; imports +28.0%; deficit ¥1,106bn, consensus ¥250bn (Trading Economics via Kaohoon). Tests US tariff exposure, autos and China demand"},{"date":"2026-10-23","event":"National CPI (Sep); flash PMIs (Oct)","why":"Core CPI prior 1.7%, consensus 2.1% (Trading Economics); headline prior 1.9%, consensus 2.3% (Newsquawk sees 2.8% to 3.0% as energy subsidies fade); core-core prior 1.9%; Tokyo already printed 2.7%. Flash PMI priors 54.1 and 51.3, consensus 54.5 and 51.3. Last major data before the 30 Oct BoJ meeting"},{"date":"2026-10-28","event":"FOMC decision (27–28 Oct meeting)","why":"Markets price about 85% for a hold in October and about 77% for a December hike after US CPI of 3.7% and PPI of 6.3%; the US 10-year is near 5.12%. Sets the yen's rate gap"},{"date":"2026-10-29","event":"Consumer confidence (Oct); 2-year JGB auction","why":"Confidence prior 35.4, consensus 35.4; 2-year prior auction yield 1.964%"},{"date":"2026-10-30","event":"BoJ decision and Outlook Report; Tokyo CPI (Oct); unemployment and jobs ratio (Sep); IP prelim, retail sales and housing starts (Sep)","why":"Bloomberg's implied October hike probability about 6% (20 Oct) after the 13 Oct report that officials lean against a back-to-back move; Ueda's 19 Oct testimony ties hikes to long-end stability; watch the FY2026 and FY2027 core CPI forecasts. Tokyo core prior 2.7%; unemployment prior 2.5%, TE forecast 2.6%; jobs ratio prior 1.18; IP prior +2.7% y/y (final), TE forecast +3.1%; retail prior +2.7%, TE forecast +5.8%; housing starts prior +6.1%, TE forecast +5.5%"},{"date":"2026-11-02","event":"Manufacturing PMI final (Oct); 5-year climate transition JGB auction; Outlook Report full text","why":"After the 54.1 September print"},{"date":"2026-11-03","event":"US midterm elections","why":"US fiscal path and tariff policy"},{"date":"2026-11-04","event":"BoJ balance sheet (end-Oct)","why":"September was ¥625tn after a quarter-end dip; purchases run at ¥2.3tn a month this quarter"},{"date":"2026-11-05","event":"Services PMI final (Oct); BoJ minutes of the 17–18 Sep meeting; 10-year JGB auction","why":"Services prior 51.3; the earthquake-disruption test. The minutes cover the 7–2 hike vote. Prior 10-year auction yield 3.101%"},{"date":"2026-11-06","event":"Real wages and cash earnings (Sep)","why":"Nominal pay prior +3.8%, TE forecast +4.0%; real wages prior +1.5%, an eighth straight gain"},{"date":"2026-11-16","event":"Q3 GDP first estimate","why":"Q2 was +1.4% annualized; TE forecast 0.6%"},{"date":"2026-11-17","event":"Trade balance and exports (Oct)","why":"Follows the 21 Oct September print; first full month after the October flash PMIs"}],"cb":{"label":"Tightening","note":"Hiked 25 bp to 1.25% on 18 Sep by 7–2; Governor Ueda told parliament on 19 Oct that underlying inflation is 'quite close' to 2%, that anchoring it around the target is becoming more important, and that failing to adjust the policy rate appropriately could push up long-term yields by stoking overshoot concerns, again without timing. Bloomberg's 13 Oct report that officials lean against a back-to-back hike on 30 Oct still stands (implied October hike probability about 6% on 20 Oct per Bloomberg via Bloomingbit; OIS about 88% by December as of 13 Oct). The 29–30 Oct meeting brings a new Outlook Report."},"cbName":"BoJ stance","conflicts":[{"a":"Tankan at +24, manufacturing PMI at 54.1 and August core machinery orders +18.6% y/y say industry and capex are strong","b":"August industrial production was +2.7% y/y and −2.4% m/m, services PMI slipped to 51.3, consumer confidence is 35.4, household spending fell 3.1% and the August tertiary index fell 0.1% m/m","read":"Exports (+19.3%) and AI-chip demand carry manufacturers' orders and sentiment while actual output fell two months running on autos and capacity utilization dropped to 103.2 from 107.7, and households face real retail growth under 1% with a ninth straight fall in spending. The tertiary index beat (−0.1% against −0.4%) says services are soft, not falling. The economy is export- and capex-led, not consumption-led, and output lags the order book.","trigger":"October flash PMIs on 23 Oct (consensus 54.5 manufacturing, 51.3 services): services back above 52 would confirm the gap is earthquake noise. September industrial production on 30 Oct (Trading Economics forecast +3.1% y/y) and October confidence on 29 Oct (consensus 35.4)."},{"a":"Ueda told parliament on 19 Oct that underlying inflation is 'quite close' to 2% and that under-adjusting the policy rate could push up long-term yields; Koeda said on 15 Oct the Bank should not be overly cautious","b":"Bloomberg's implied probability of an October hike is about 6%, officials were reported on 13 Oct to lean against a back-to-back move, Ueda gave no timing, and the Nikkei is 11.1% off its high","read":"The Governor and the board's most explicit hawk agree on direction while the sourcing says October is a pause with hawkish hints. Core CPI at 1.7% is below target only because of subsidies while Tokyo core jumped to 2.7%, so the Bank is hiking on underlying inflation, not the headline. Brent near $110 adds an upside price risk that argues for sooner; the equity drawdown argues for waiting until the Outlook Report.","trigger":"National September CPI on 23 Oct: a core print at or above the 2.1% consensus revives October odds and cements December. The 30 Oct Outlook Report's FY2027 core CPI forecast and any reference to oil or equity volatility."},{"a":"Real wages are positive for an eighth month and nominal pay is up 3.8%","b":"The jobs ratio has fallen to 1.18 from 1.35 and unemployment ticked up to 2.5%","read":"Wage gains come from the shunto (5%-plus for a third year) and labor shortage, not from rising labor demand. If the real gain is only a subsidy effect, the virtuous cycle stalls when subsidies end.","trigger":"September labor data on 30 Oct (unemployment consensus 2.5%) and September real wages on 6 Nov; a jobs ratio below 1.15 would signal real softening."},{"a":"Takaichi said on 19 Oct she would not rule out bonds to fund the ¥10tn food tax cut, FY2027 budget requests are a record ¥143tn and Kyodo approval fell to 49.1%","b":"The 10-year JGB fell to 2.914% on 19 Oct, the 30-year is 3.99% after a record 4.235% on 5 Oct, and the 20-year auction on 20 Oct drew a record 5.84 bid-to-cover at 3.723% with a 0.07 tail","read":"The market is pricing the extra issuance in the level of yields, not in demand: the 30-year is still about 85 bp above a year ago, but at these levels life insurers (Fukoku) are rotating from foreign bonds into JGBs and every October auction has cleared with above-average cover. The October fall in yields is global risk-off and lower US yields plus that demand, not a verdict that the plan is funded. Weaker political capital makes it harder to fund the cut with spending cuts.","trigger":"The 2-year auction on 29 Oct, the 10-year on 5 Nov and the FY2027 budget draft in December. A 30-year close above 4.30% with a tail wider than 0.3 would mark a demand problem; a 10-year back above 3.10% while equities fall would say the fiscal premium is back."},{"a":"TOPIX held 4,067.48 (+0.04%) on 19 Oct and is 1.7% above its 15 Oct close, the Philadelphia semiconductor index rose 1.6% after the Tokyo close and TSMC guided Q4 revenue to US$52.3bn to US$54.3bn","b":"The Nikkei fell 0.62% to 64,916.70 after a 2% intraday drop, Prime breadth was 1,413 up and 2,064 down, and Brent settled at $109.55","read":"The selling is aimed at AI financing and software multiples, not at chip demand, which TSMC's guidance says is intact, nor at Japanese fundamentals. TOPIX is flat on the week-start while the Nikkei keeps bleeding, so the index overstates the damage, but breadth of two decliners per gainer says the broad market is only holding, not recovering, and $110 oil adds a macro reason to sell an energy importer.","trigger":"US AI earnings this week and the 20 Oct Tokyo close: a Nikkei close above 66,000 with TOPIX above 4,050 would mark the 15 Oct low (64,425.95) as a washout; a Nikkei close below 64,425.95 or a TOPIX close below 3,978 would say the weakness has broadened beyond chips. Japanese earnings season from late October."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-10-20","impact":"Super-long demand is strong at yields near 30-year highs; the 20-year yield fell sharply after the result and the fiscal-stress reading eases for now.","src":"Newsquawk; Bloomberg via Bloomingbit; Reuters headline via MarketScreener","tag":"Markets","title":"20-year JGB auction draws a record 5.84 bid-to-cover (prior 4.01) at an average 3.723%, tail 0.07","url":"https://www.newsquawk.com/headlines/japan-sells-jpy-700bln-20yr-jgb-bc-584x-prev-401x-average-yield-37230-prev-38560-tail-007-prev-015"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"Confirms the hiking bias without timing; the market keeps October at about 6% and looks to December for a move to 1.5%.","src":"Reuters via US News; The Edge Malaysia; ActionForex","tag":"Policy","title":"Ueda tells parliament underlying inflation is 'quite close' to 2%; under-adjusting the policy rate could push up long-term yields","url":"https://theedgemalaysia.com/node/822296"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"First admission that the ¥10tn cut may be debt-financed; JGB yields still fell on the day as risk aversion dominated.","src":"Reuters via Arab News Japan and Business Today; Nippon.com","tag":"Politics","title":"Takaichi: 'would not rule out' bond issuance to fund the food consumption tax cut, will control annual issuance 'appropriately'","url":"https://www.arabnews.jp/en/business/japan-pm-says-wont-rule-out-debt-issuance-to-fund-tax-cut-3000922"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"The 16 Oct bounce did not extend; chip names led the losses while value and energy held the broad index.","src":"Reuters via Business Recorder and MarketScreener; UCapital; Investing.com; kabutore.biz","tag":"Markets","title":"Nikkei closes down 0.62% at 64,916.70 after falling more than 2% intraday; TOPIX flat at 4,067.48","url":"https://www.brecorder.com/news/40445028/nikkei-slides-as-concerns-mount-before-tech-results"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"Services activity held up better than feared after the 51.3 services PMI; a small positive for the coincident read.","src":"METI via RTTNews and Newsquawk","tag":"Data","title":"August tertiary industry index −0.1% m/m against −0.4% expected, after +0.2% in July","url":"https://www.rttnews.com/story.aspx?Id=3699990"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"A better lead for Tokyo on 20 Oct; oil stays near $110 after Houthi strikes on Saudi energy sites and Hormuz flows of about 8.5 million b/d.","src":"Reuters via Newsy Today; TheStreet; Reuters via Yahoo Finance (oil)","tag":"Markets","title":"Wall Street steadies: Nasdaq +1.11%, S&P 500 +0.41% to 7,690.73, Philadelphia semiconductor index +1.6% after an eight-day slide; Brent settles at $109.55","url":"https://www.thestreet.com/stock-market-today/stock-market-today-oct-19-2026-nasdaq-futures-climb-as-tech-rebound-from-sell-off"},{"date":"2026-10-18","impact":"Less political capital to fund the tax cut with spending cuts before the FY2027 budget.","src":"Kyodo via Japan Times and Alo Japan","tag":"Politics","title":"Kyodo poll: Takaichi cabinet approval falls 4.3 points to 49.1%, the lowest since taking office","url":"https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2026/10/18/japan/politics/takaichis-support-rate-falls/"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"The rebound recovered part of the 15 Oct 3,318-point fall: intraday high 65,550.68, Prime breadth 1,292 up and 224 down, 32 of 33 sectors higher, TOPIX +66.66 (+1.67%, −0.95% on the week). Tokyo Electron added about 134 index points and SoftBank Group about 74 (+1.90% to ¥4,944), Kioxia rose 3.84% to ¥13,375, BayCurrent 17.25% on a buyback plan, Kawasaki Heavy 6.41%; Toho −2.34% and Ryohin Keikaku −1.98% led the losers as defensives were sold. The Nikkei volatility index fell to 32.17 from 36.13. Daiwa's Hashizume said the gains followed the prior day's losses and nothing else, and that investors see little reason to buy aggressively before earnings and the 3 Nov US midterms. JGBs gave back some of the haven bid: 10-year 2.937%, 2-year 1.92% (+1.5 bp), 20-year 3.815% (+3 bp), 30-year 4.002%. USD/JPY closed at 158.00.","src":"Nikkei Indexes; Nikkei wire via BigGo Finance; News On Japan; Reuters via Business Recorder; TradingKey; Investing.com","tag":"Markets","title":"Nikkei closes up 1.39% at 65,324.29, snapping six straight losses, but drops 5.37% on the week; TOPIX back above 4,000 at 4,066.00","url":"https://indexes.nikkei.co.jp/en/nkave"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"Speaking after the IMF meetings, Governor Ueda said underlying inflation is hovering around 2%, that the Bank will continue to raise rates and aim to keep underlying inflation near target, that it cannot allow it to climb further, and that recent domestic data and talks with global policymakers show prices and the economy broadly tracking the Bank's projections. The board will consider how to keep inflation anchored at the 29–30 Oct meeting, which brings new growth and inflation forecasts. He gave no signal on timing, so the 13 Oct Bloomberg read (October pause, December live) stands. The 2-year JGB rose 1.5 bp to 1.92% and the yen was flat near 158.","src":"Reuters via Rallies; Finimize; Bloomberg (headline)","tag":"Policy","title":"Ueda in Bangkok: the BoJ 'cannot tolerate' inflation rising further above 2% and will keep raising rates","url":"https://rallies.ai/news/boj-chief-vows-to-keep-raising-rates-anchor-inflation-around-target-d8753df5677db5a2"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"The S&P 500 closed at 7,659.44 (−1.09%, −1.9% on the week), the Nasdaq at 26,140.03 (−2.14%, −4.5% on the week, the worst since late June), the Dow at 52,002.41 (−0.34%) and the Russell 2000 at 2,721.37 (−1.7%). Technology fell more than 3% and energy rose 2.52%; 41 of 45 software names in Yahoo Finance's basket fell, erasing about $130bn, CoreWeave fell about 12% on its debt load, Palantir about 7% and Meta about 3.7%, while the semiconductor ETF headed for a ninth straight decline. The US 10-year was about 5.07% to 5.10%. October Fed hike odds fell to about 15% from 33% after Wednesday's CPI, with one 25 bp hike still priced by December. A weak lead for the Nikkei's 19 Oct open after its 1.39% rebound.","src":"AP via ABC News; Yahoo Finance; TheStreet","tag":"Markets","title":"Wall Street ends the week with another tech selloff: Nasdaq −2.14%, S&P 500 −1.09%, CoreWeave about −12%","url":"https://abcnews.com/Business/wireStory/major-us-stock-indexes-fared-friday-10162026-137331951"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"Brent rose 3.7% and WTI 3.5% to $101.41 for a second straight weekly gain. Houthi strikes on Saudi Arabia continue, Trump did not rule out heavier strikes on Iran after the 3 Nov election, Eurasia Group raised its odds of conflict intensification to 65% from 35%, Ukraine hit Russia's Ryazan refinery and US lawmakers moved to block a Russian diesel release; the US offered up to 17 million barrels from the SPR. Hormuz cargo volumes had been near pre-war levels at over 100 million barrels of crude last week. Japan takes about 96% of its crude from the Middle East, so $110 oil widens the trade deficit, leans on the yen, lifts headline CPI once subsidies lapse and cuts household real income.","src":"Rigzone; Yahoo Finance","tag":"Geopolitics","title":"Brent settles at $109.86, the first touch of $110 since May, as Iran escalates attacks around Hormuz","url":"https://www.rigzone.com/news/brent_oil_hits_110_as_supply_risks_mount-16-oct-2026-184870-article"},{"date":"2026-10-15","impact":"Speaking after the G20 finance meeting on the sidelines of the IMF annual meetings, the finance minister said she told counterparts that financial market developments must be watched closely with a high sense of urgency, and the G20 reaffirmed its commitment that excessive volatility and disorderly FX moves are undesirable, which Tokyo cites as cover for intervention. Asked about rising long-term rates she said each country should pursue fiscal sustainability while using fiscal room for growth investment. Officials worry a weak yen raises fuel import costs during the Middle East war. USD/JPY closed at 158.14 that day and 158.00 on 16 Oct.","src":"Reuters via MarketScreener; Japan Times","tag":"Policy","title":"Katayama tells G20 to watch FX 'with a high sense of urgency'; no communiqué from the Bangkok meeting","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/japan-tells-g20-must-watch-fx-moves-with-high-sense-of-urgency-ce785dd3dc80f02d"},{"date":"2026-10-15","impact":"Kazuo Yana, appointed in the 17 Sep reshuffle, quit on 15 Oct after saying road budgets were cut for municipalities whose mayors did not back him, a retracted remark, and amid reports of free campaign shuttle buses and a paid supporter in 2024 and 2026 that may breach the election law; Xinhua described it as effectively a dismissal. NHK's latest poll shows the cabinet's approval at its lowest since Takaichi took office, an Asahi poll found 76% see the repeated unfunded food-tax-cut promise as problematic and 68% object to appointing lawmakers tied to the slush-fund scandal, and a BoJ survey found nearly 90% of households expect prices to keep rising. Weaker political capital complicates funding the ¥10tn tax cut through spending cuts before the FY2027 budget.","src":"Xinhua","tag":"Politics","title":"Farm minister Yana resigns after less than a month; NHK poll puts Takaichi cabinet approval at its lowest","url":"https://english.news.cn/20261016/fad75a398f844c47b7a17471ab88ea6d/c.html"},{"date":"2026-10-15","impact":"The third-largest point drop on record followed a 4%-plus fall in the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index, CoreWeave bonds at distressed levels, US CPI of 3.7% and Koeda's hawkish remarks; the KOSPI fell nearly 8% and triggered a circuit breaker. Advantest fell 14.96% and took about 1,300 points off the index, SUMCO about 14%, Lasertec about 13%, with Kioxia, Tokyo Electron, Ibiden, SoftBank Group and Fujikura also lower; Sumitomo Pharma rose 15.81%, Ryohin Keikaku more than 9% and Takeda about 5% in a rotation to defensives. Prime breadth 564 up, 948 down, turnover ¥10.14tn. TOPIX fell 97.44 points (−2.38%) with MUFG −1.6%, SMFG −1.7% and Mizuho −2.7%. JGBs rallied: 10-year 2.911% at the close (Reuters 2.93% intraday), 2-year 1.912%, 5-year 2.325%, 20-year 3.80%, 30-year 3.995%. USD/JPY closed at 158.14 after dipping to 157.55. Finance Minister Katayama said G7 and G20 finance ministers see no financial-stability threat from the AI sell-off and renewed the warning that Tokyo is ready to act in FX if needed.","src":"Nikkei Indexes; News On Japan; Moomoo (Nikkei wire); Trading Economics; Reuters via Rallies; investing.com; Crypto Briefing","tag":"Markets","title":"Nikkei closes down 4.90% at 64,425.95, a 3,318-point fall and sixth straight loss; TOPIX ends below 4,000 at 3,999.34","url":"https://newsonjapan.com/article/151072.php"},{"date":"2026-10-15","impact":"Revenue was NT$1,494.2bn (+50.9% y/y in NT$, +17.6% q/q), gross margin 66.4%, operating margin 58.7% and 2-nanometre already 8% of wafer revenue. Q4 guidance of US$52.3bn to US$54.3bn with a 65% to 67% gross margin says leading-edge demand is intact, which matters for Japanese equipment and materials names (Tokyo Electron, Advantest, Lasertec, SUMCO) that fell 13% to 15% the same day on AI-financing fears. TSMC shares eased 0.4% on 16 Oct.","src":"TSMC 6-K filing (SEC)","tag":"Earnings","title":"TSMC posts record Q3: revenue US$46.72bn (+41.2% y/y), net income NT$763.1bn (+68.7%), Q4 guidance US$52.3bn to US$54.3bn","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001046179/000104617926000726/a3q26e_withguidancexfinal.htm"},{"date":"2026-10-15","impact":"September PPI rose 1.3% m/m against 0.5% expected and 6.3% y/y against 5.4%, with core +0.5% m/m; retail sales rose 0.8% (control group +0.1%) mostly on gasoline; the Philadelphia Fed index fell to 20.9 from 37.8 while Empire State jumped to 25.5; jobless claims 199k. Despite the hot print the US 10-year fell about 6 bp to about 5.09% (intraday low about 5.04%), the dollar index fell 0.5% to about 102.0, the S&P 500 rose 0.3% to about 7,740 with the equal-weight index +1.4%, WTI rose 2.2% to about $98 and gold 1.6% to about $4,170. Fed's Daly said the Fed does not need to move in October; Goolsbee said AI data-centre demand could warrant a stronger response. USD/JPY fell toward 157.6 with yields, then climbed back above 158.","src":"babypips; Investing.com","tag":"Data","title":"US PPI jumps to 6.3% y/y and retail sales +0.8%, yet Treasury yields fall and the dollar slips","url":"https://www.babypips.com/news/financial-forex-market-recap-2026-10-15"}],"headline":"The Nikkei fell 0.62% to 64,916.70 on 19 Oct after an intraday drop of more than 2% while TOPIX held flat at 4,067.48; the 10-year JGB eased to 2.914%, the 30-year to 3.99% and USD/JPY closed at 158.05 as Ueda told parliament underlying inflation is 'quite close' to 2% and Takaichi said she would not rule out bonds to fund the food tax cut; on 20 Oct the 20-year JGB auction drew a record 5.84 bid-to-cover at 3.723%.","marketClose":"Mon 19 Oct 2026","notes":["19 Oct closes: Nikkei 64,916.70 (−407.59, −0.62%) from Reuters via Business Recorder and UCapital; Investing.com's close report said −0.46% without a level and is not used. TOPIX 4,067.48 (+1.48, +0.04%) from kabutore.biz, matching Reuters. Breadth figures are Investing.com (Prime: 1,413 up, 2,064 down, 254 unchanged) and Reuters (Nikkei 225: 111 up, 108 down).","JGB yields: the 19 Oct 10-year close is 2.914% (investing.com; the same table now shows 16 Oct at 2.935% where the previous refresh recorded 2.937%). The 2-year 1.90%, 20-year 3.78% and 30-year 3.99% are Trading Economics closes for 19 Oct; Reuters' morning quotes were 1.89%, 3.79% and 'unchanged' for the 30-year. The MoF reference-rate CSV was reachable this run but ends at 16 Oct, where it shows 10-year 2.958%, 2-year 1.918%, 20-year 3.803% and 30-year 4.040%, 2 to 4 bp above the table closes used in these rows; the rows keep the investing.com and Trading Economics basis for continuity.","20-year auction figures (5.84 bid-to-cover, 3.723% average, 0.07 tail, ¥700bn) are Newsquawk and Bloomberg via Bloomingbit for 20 Oct; the post-auction yield levels were not captured because the 20 Oct session was open at refresh time. Market rows cover the 19 Oct close.","USD/JPY 158.05 is the investing.com table close for 19 Oct (range 157.70 to 158.13); investing.com's equities report showed 157.92 and UCapital 158.03. investing.com's FX closes shift with the session cut-off (16 Oct now shows 158.01), so day-to-day changes in this row are approximate.","The only Japanese data on 19 Oct was the August tertiary industry index (−0.1% m/m, RTTNews and Newsquawk); it has no row, so all indicator rows and their periods are unchanged. Industrial production August is the 15 Oct final (+2.7% y/y, −2.4% m/m); machinery orders August (+18.6% y/y) is the 15 Oct Cabinet Office release via FX.co and RTTNews.","Ueda's 19 Oct remarks are Reuters via US News and The Edge Malaysia plus ActionForex; the committee is not named. The 'about 6%' October hike probability is Bloomberg's implied figure as relayed by Bloomingbit on 20 Oct; the 88% December figure is OIS as of 13 Oct and was not re-quoted. Fed odds (October hold about 85%, December hike about 77%, US 10-year about 5.12%) are Trading Economics' 19 Oct note.","US figures for 19 Oct: S&P 500 7,690.73 (+0.41%), Nasdaq 26,430.89 (+1.11%), Dow 51,966.03 (−0.07%) from Reuters via Newsy Today, matching TheStreet's percentages; 24/7 Wall St. showed 7,709.63 (+0.66%) and is not used. Brent $109.55 and WTI $103.66 (November, expiring 20 Oct; December $98.54) are Reuters settlements via Yahoo Finance.","CPI consensus: Trading Economics (via Kaohoon) 2.1% core and 2.3% headline; Newsquawk's week-ahead puts headline at 2.8% to 3.0% with core rising 'significantly'. Both are shown. Calendar and next-release dates are Tokyo dates. Final PMI dates (2 Nov manufacturing, 5 Nov services) follow the S&P Global release calendar.","The 10-year JGB history before September 2026 is monthly averages (FRED), not month-end closes; the 2-year and 30-year rows show daily closes since 30 Sep because month-end history is unavailable; the TOPIX history is quarter-end closes (see history.json notes).","'Highest since 2018' for the Tankan is based on the MQL5 history table, where +24 last appeared in the March 2018 survey."],"posture":{"label":"Risk-off","note":"The 16 Oct bounce did not hold: the Nikkei fell 0.62% to 64,916.70 on 19 Oct after being down more than 2% intraday, while TOPIX was flat at 4,067.48 (+0.04%), so the Neutral test (Nikkei above 66,000 and TOPIX above 4,050 at a close) is still half met and the index is 11.1% below the June high. Prime breadth was 1,413 up and 2,064 down. After the Tokyo close Wall Street steadied (Nasdaq +1.11%, Philadelphia semiconductor index +1.6% ending an eight-day losing streak) and Brent settled at $109.55, so the 20 Oct session opened with a better lead and the 20-year JGB auction drew a record 5.84 bid-to-cover. 10-year JGB 2.914%, 30-year 3.99%, USD/JPY 158.05."},"regime":{"label":"Expansion","note":"Manufacturing PMI 54.1, Tankan large manufacturers +24 (best since 2018), exports +19.3%, real wages +1.5% and August core machinery orders +18.6% y/y keep the leading side positive; the coincident side is softer with August industrial production at +2.7% y/y (−2.4% m/m), services PMI 51.3, consumer confidence 35.4 and household spending −3.1%. The only new data on 19 Oct was the August tertiary industry index at −0.1% m/m against −0.4% expected. The next tests are September exports on 21 Oct and national CPI with the October flash PMIs on 23 Oct (core CPI consensus 2.1%, manufacturing PMI 54.5)."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"−0.8","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":48.2},{"l":"Nov 25","v":48.7},{"l":"Dec 25","v":50},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":53},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":55.1},{"l":"May 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":54.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":54.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":54.1}],"latest":"54.1","name":"au Jibun Bank Manufacturing PMI","next":"23 Oct (Oct flash)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"54.9","read":"A ninth month above 50 and a seven-month low. Output and new orders grew more slowly, export orders rose again but less than August's eight-and-a-half-year high, and input cost inflation eased to a six-month low. August was revised down to 54.9 from 55.1.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion","src":"S&P Global via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Easing","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/manufacturing-pmi"},{"change":"−1.2","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":53.1},{"l":"Nov 25","v":53.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":51.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":53.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":53.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":51},{"l":"May 26","v":50},{"l":"Jun 26","v":52.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":51.3}],"latest":"51.3","name":"au Jibun Bank Services PMI","next":"23 Oct (Oct flash)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"52.5","read":"Final reading cut from the 51.6 flash. New orders slowed, partly on disruption after the 28 July Kumamoto earthquake, and export orders fell sharply. Selling prices rose at one of the fastest rates on record, so the divergence with manufacturing (54.1) is in demand, not prices.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion, slower","src":"S&P Global via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slowing","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/services-pmi"},{"change":"+2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 23","v":12},{"l":"Mar 24","v":11},{"l":"Jun 24","v":13},{"l":"Sep 24","v":13},{"l":"Dec 24","v":14},{"l":"Mar 25","v":12},{"l":"Jun 25","v":13},{"l":"Sep 25","v":14},{"l":"Dec 25","v":15},{"l":"Mar 26","v":17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":22},{"l":"Sep 26","v":24}],"latest":"+24","name":"Tankan Large Manufacturers DI","next":"mid-Dec (Dec survey)","period":"Sep 2026 survey","prior":"+22","read":"Released 1 Oct. Large manufacturers improved for a sixth quarter to +24, the best since March 2018, while large non-manufacturers slipped to +35 from +37. The December outlook DI is +21 for manufacturers and +30 for non-manufacturers, so firms expect some cooling. FY2026 capex plans for large firms are +11.3%.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = favorable equals unfavorable","signal":"good","signalWord":"Highest since 2018","src":"Bank of Japan","trend":"up","trendWord":"Improving","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/statistics/tk/yoshi/tk2609.htm"},{"change":"−0.1","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":35.8},{"l":"Nov 25","v":37.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":37.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":37.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":40},{"l":"Mar 26","v":33.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":32.2},{"l":"May 26","v":33.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":33.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":34.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":35.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":35.4}],"latest":"35.4","name":"Consumer Confidence Index","next":"29 Oct","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"35.5","read":"Sideways at the mid-30s after the March drop from 40.0 to 33.3 when the Hormuz closure lifted fuel prices. Livelihood (33.8) and durable-goods willingness (27.3) weakened; income growth (40.5) and employment (40.0) edged up. October consensus is 35.4.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = neutral line","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 40 band","src":"Cabinet Office via Trading Economics","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Flat","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"+7.4 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":11.6},{"l":"Oct 25","v":12.5},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-6.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":16.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":13.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":24.7},{"l":"Mar 26","v":5.9},{"l":"Apr 26","v":15.6},{"l":"May 26","v":-1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":16.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":11.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":18.6}],"latest":"+18.6%","name":"Core Machinery Orders, y/y","next":"mid-Nov (Sep)","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"+11.2%","read":"Released 15 Oct. Orders rose 5.2% on the month to ¥1,069.8bn against a +3.0% consensus, reversing July's −3.7%, and 18.6% above a year ago against +15.7% expected. Manufacturing orders rose 1.6% m/m to ¥527.0bn and non-manufacturing ex utilities 2.9% to ¥541.3bn. The series is noisy; the Tankan capex plans (+11.3% for large firms) are the cleaner read and point the same way.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Capex orders beat","src":"Cabinet Office via FX.co and RTTNews","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebounding","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3225835"},{"change":"−2.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":-7.3},{"l":"Oct 25","v":3.2},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-8.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-1.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-0.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-4.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-29.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":11.4},{"l":"May 26","v":33.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":18.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":8.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.1}],"latest":"+6.1%","name":"Housing Starts, y/y","next":"30 Oct (Sep)","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"+8.2%","read":"Fourth straight positive print, but the gains follow the April 2025 to Feb 2026 slump (−26.6% to −34.4% in spring 2025) after the building-code change, so the base is low. Growth has slowed from +33.9% in May. With 10-year JGBs above 3% and mortgage rates rising, the underlying trend is flat at best.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Base-effect rebound","src":"MLIT via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Fading","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/housing-starts"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"−1.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":3.8},{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2},{"l":"May 26","v":-2.1},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.7}],"latest":"+2.7%","name":"Industrial Production, y/y","next":"30 Oct (Sep prelim)","period":"Aug 2026 (final y/y)","prior":"+3.9%","read":"The 15 Oct final cut August to +2.7% y/y from the +3.4% preliminary and to −2.4% m/m from −1.7%, the second straight monthly decline and the steepest since February, led by motor vehicles (−6.8%), general-purpose machinery (−6.0%) and petroleum products (−13.3%). Manufacturing is +3.4% y/y, mining −5.2% and capacity utilization fell to 103.2 from 107.7. The y/y run is positive for an eighth month but output is lagging the order book, and Trading Economics forecasts +3.1% y/y for September on 30 Oct.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Slowing, two monthly falls","src":"METI via Trading Economics and babypips","trend":"down","trendWord":"Revised down","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/industrial-production"},{"change":"−1.0 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":0.5},{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-0.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-0.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.1},{"l":"May 26","v":5.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":0.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.7}],"latest":"+2.7%","name":"Retail Sales, y/y","next":"30 Oct (Sep)","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"+3.7%","read":"Below the 3.3% consensus, with sales down 1.2% on the month. The gain is mostly motor vehicles (+17.7%); with CPI at 1.9%, real retail growth is under 1%. Sixth straight y/y gain. July was revised down to 3.7% from 4.0%.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Missed consensus","src":"METI via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slowing","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/retail-sales-annual"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.5},{"l":"May 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.5}],"latest":"2.5%","name":"Unemployment Rate","next":"30 Oct (Sep)","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"2.4%","read":"Up from July's 12-month low and a touch above the 2.4% consensus. Unemployed rose 100K to 1.77M while employment rose 300K to 68.34M. The range since 2022 is 2.4% to 2.8%. September consensus is 2.5%.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Tight","src":"MIC via Trading Economics; history FRED","trend":"up","trendWord":"Edging up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/unemployment-rate"},{"change":"0.00","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":1.2},{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.18},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1.18},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.19},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.19},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.18},{"l":"May 26","v":1.17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.18}],"latest":"1.18","name":"Jobs-to-Applicants Ratio","next":"30 Oct (Sep)","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"1.18","read":"Held at 1.18 for a third month, below the 1.2 level our framework treats as tight and down from 1.35 in late 2022. Openings still exceed applicants, so wage pressure persists, but demand for labor has been easing for three years. The Tankan employment DI (−38 all firms) says shortages remain severe.","ref":1,"refLabel":"1.0 = one opening per applicant","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 1.2 line","src":"MHLW via Trading Economics","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Flat","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/unemployment-rate"},{"change":"−3.9 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":4.2},{"l":"Oct 25","v":3.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":6.1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":5.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":16.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":4.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":11.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":14.8},{"l":"May 26","v":17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":19.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":23.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":19.3}],"latest":"+19.3%","name":"Exports, y/y","next":"21 Oct (Sep)","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"+23.2%","read":"Above the 18.2% consensus. Semiconductors +52.3%, electrical machinery +31.5%, exports to the US +24.9% and to China +20.6%. Motor vehicles +16.5% under the 15% US auto tariff in force since 16 Sep 2025. Imports +28.0% on oil, so the trade deficit widened to ¥1,106bn.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Chips and US demand","src":"MoF trade statistics; Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Still strong","url":"https://www.customs.go.jp/toukei/shinbun/trade-st_e/2026/2026084e.pdf"},{"change":"−0.9 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":-1.4},{"l":"Oct 25","v":-0.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-0.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.9},{"l":"May 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.5}],"latest":"+1.5%","name":"Real Wages, y/y","next":"6 Nov (Sep)","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"+2.4%","read":"Eighth straight gain, in line with forecasts. Nominal cash earnings rose 3.8%, a seventh month above 3%, after the 2026 shunto delivered a third year of 5%-plus settlements. The positive real print leans on subsidy-depressed CPI (1.9% headline), which is why the BoJ reads it cautiously.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = wages keep pace with prices","signal":"good","signalWord":"Positive 8 months","src":"MHLW via Japan Times, MacroStream","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slower growth","url":"https://www.japantimes.co.jp/business/2026/10/07/economy/takaichi-real-wages/"},{"change":"−0.5 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 23","v":-4.9},{"l":"Dec 23","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 24","v":-0.4},{"l":"Jun 24","v":-0.5},{"l":"Sep 24","v":3.4},{"l":"Dec 24","v":1.6},{"l":"Mar 25","v":2},{"l":"Jun 25","v":0.5},{"l":"Sep 25","v":-1.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.1},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.4}],"latest":"+1.4%","name":"Real GDP, q/q annualized","next":"16 Nov (Q3 prelim)","period":"Q2 2026 (2nd prelim)","prior":"+1.9%","read":"Revised up from the 1.1% first estimate on 8 Sep. Government spending and net trade carried the quarter; private consumption was subdued by energy costs and capex subtracted for a second quarter. Q3 first estimate is due 16 Nov; Trading Economics forecasts 0.6%.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no growth","signal":"good","signalWord":"Third straight gain","src":"Cabinet Office ESRI; Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slower","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/gdp-growth-annualized"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"−0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":2.9},{"l":"Oct 25","v":3},{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.5},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.4},{"l":"May 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.7}],"latest":"1.7%","name":"Core CPI (ex fresh food), y/y","next":"23 Oct (Sep)","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"1.8%","read":"Eighth month below the 2% target and under the 1.8% consensus. Headline is 1.9%. Fuel and utility subsidies reinstated on 19 Mar are holding the index down while crude is near $100; the BoJ says underlying inflation is approaching 2% and could overshoot. September is due 23 Oct; Trading Economics forecasts 1.9%.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoJ target","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 2%, subsidy-held","src":"MIC via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Easing","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"0.0 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":3},{"l":"Oct 25","v":3.1},{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.9},{"l":"May 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.9}],"latest":"1.9%","name":"Core-core CPI (ex food and energy), y/y","next":"23 Oct (Sep)","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"1.9%","read":"Unchanged at 1.9%, the fastest in three months, after slowing from 3.4% in mid-2025. This is the measure least affected by subsidies and it sits just under target, which supports the BoJ's claim that wage pass-through is still working.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2%","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Near 2%","src":"MIC via Trading Economics","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Steady","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"+0.9 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.8},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.5},{"l":"May 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.7}],"latest":"2.7%","name":"Tokyo Core CPI, y/y","next":"30 Oct (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"1.8%","read":"The early read for the nation jumped to 2.7% against a 2.4% consensus, a fourth straight acceleration and the fastest since November 2025, back above 2% for the first time in nine months. Ex food and energy rose to 3.0% from 2.0%. Higher oil prices from the Middle East conflict are passing through despite larger gasoline subsidies.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoJ target","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Oil pass-through","src":"MIC via Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Jumping","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/tokyo-core-cpi"},{"change":"+25 bp","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":0.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":0.75},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.75},{"l":"May 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.25},{"l":"19 Oct","v":1.25}],"latest":"1.25%","name":"BoJ Policy Rate","next":"30 Oct","period":"Since 24 Sep","prior":"1.00%","read":"Raised on 18 Sep by 7–2 (Asada and Sato dissenting), the second hike in three months after 16 Jun, and the highest since 1995. The statement says the Bank 'will continue to raise the policy interest rate'. Next meeting 29–30 Oct with a new Outlook Report. On 13 Oct Bloomberg reported that officials are leaning against a back-to-back hike in October unless upside price risks flare, and will judge December on the data; OIS priced about 10% for October and about 88% for a hike by December. On 15 Oct board member Koeda said the Bank should not become overly cautious about raising rates. On 16 Oct Ueda said in Bangkok that the Bank cannot tolerate inflation rising further above 2%, and on 19 Oct he told parliament that underlying inflation is 'quite close' to 2%, that strong global AI demand is adding to price pressure, and that failing to adjust the policy rate appropriately could push up long-term rates; emergency bond buying is reserved for sharp yield rises. Bloomberg's implied October hike probability was about 6% on 20 Oct.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Tightening","src":"Bank of Japan; Bloomberg via Briefs and PANews; Reuters","trend":"up","trendWord":"Hiking","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/mopo/mpmdeci/mpr_2026/k260918a.pdf"},{"change":"−¥20tn in Sep","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":696.3},{"l":"Nov 25","v":697.9},{"l":"Dec 25","v":677.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":682.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":683.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":663},{"l":"Apr 26","v":663.3},{"l":"May 26","v":664.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":639.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":644.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":644.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":625}],"latest":"¥625tn","name":"BoJ Total Assets","next":"4 Nov (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"¥645tn","read":"Down from a ¥765tn peak in Aug 2024 as JGB purchases are cut by about ¥200bn a quarter to ¥2.3tn a month in Oct–Dec, heading to ¥2tn from April 2027. The June plan targets JGB holdings of about ¥480tn by March 2027, 17% below mid-2024. Quarter-end months dip on redemptions, so the ¥20tn fall overstates the trend.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"QT by taper","src":"BoJ via FRED JPNASSETS; BoJ purchase plan","trend":"down","trendWord":"Shrinking","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/mopo/mpmdeci/mpr_2026/k260616b.pdf"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the leading and coincident core. They are not part of the original indicator list.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"−15 bp","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":1.805},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.06},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.24},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.11},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.345},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.515},{"l":"May 26","v":2.65},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.67},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.79},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.94},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.063},{"l":"19 Oct","v":2.914}],"latest":"2.914%","name":"10-Year JGB Yield","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"3.063% on 30 Sep","read":"Closed at 2.914% on 19 Oct (investing.com, range 2.896 to 2.918), down about 2 bp as the Nikkei's early 2% drop brought a haven bid and Takaichi pledged to control annual bond issuance 'appropriately'; Reuters had the benchmark at 2.90% (−3 bp) in the morning. On 20 Oct the 20-year auction drew a record 5.84 bid-to-cover and the 20-year yield fell sharply. The yield touched about 3.12% on 6 Oct, the highest since 1996, and is still about 85 bp above the December 2025 average of 2.06% on BoJ hikes, Takaichi's fiscal plans and a US 10-year near 5.12%. Ueda's 19 Oct line that under-adjusting the policy rate would push up long-term yields frames hikes as support for the long end. MoF reference rates for 16 Oct (10-year 2.958%) run 2 to 4 bp above these table closes. Monthly points before Sep are monthly averages.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Haven bid, firm auctions","src":"investing.com; Reuters via Rallies; Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Easing","url":"https://www.investing.com/rates-bonds/japan-10-year-bond-yield-historical-data"},{"change":"−4 bp","hist":[{"l":"30 Sep","v":1.939},{"l":"6 Oct","v":1.93},{"l":"9 Oct","v":1.911},{"l":"13 Oct","v":1.918},{"l":"14 Oct","v":1.915},{"l":"15 Oct","v":1.912},{"l":"16 Oct","v":1.92},{"l":"19 Oct","v":1.9}],"latest":"1.90%","name":"2-Year JGB Yield","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"1.939% on 30 Sep","read":"Trading Economics shows 1.90% on 19 Oct, down 1 bp; Reuters quoted 1.89% (−1.5 bp) in the morning session. The yield is about 65 bp above the 1.25% policy rate and prices the next hike for December rather than October: Bloomberg's implied October probability is about 6% while Ueda's 19 Oct testimony that underlying inflation is 'quite close' to 2% keeps a December move to 1.5% as the base case. The record high was 1.98% in September after the hike. MoF's 16 Oct reference rate was 1.918%. Month-end history before September could not be confirmed.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Prices a December hike","src":"Trading Economics; Reuters via Rallies; investing.com","trend":"down","trendWord":"Easing","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/2-year-note-yield"},{"change":"−16 bp","hist":[{"l":"30 Sep","v":4.154},{"l":"2 Oct","v":4.206},{"l":"6 Oct","v":4.168},{"l":"9 Oct","v":4.06},{"l":"13 Oct","v":4.045},{"l":"15 Oct","v":3.995},{"l":"16 Oct","v":4.002},{"l":"19 Oct","v":3.99}],"latest":"3.99%","name":"30-Year JGB Yield","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"4.154% on 30 Sep","read":"Trading Economics shows 3.99% on 19 Oct, down about 1 bp (Reuters: unchanged in the morning) and 24 bp below the 5 Oct record of 4.235%; the 20-year closed at 3.78% (−3 bp) and the 40-year 4.11%. On 20 Oct the ¥700bn 20-year auction cleared at an average 3.723% with a record 5.84 bid-to-cover (prior 4.01, 12-month average 3.73) and a 0.07 tail (prior 0.15), after the 8 Oct 30-year drew 3.88 at 4.109%; Fukoku Mutual Life said higher yields let it shift from foreign bonds into yen debt. Demand is firm, so the super-long end is pricing issuance and inflation, not a buyers' strike. Takaichi's 19 Oct remark that she would not rule out bonds to fund the ¥10tn food tax cut is the standing fiscal risk; MoF's 16 Oct reference rate was 4.040%.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Fiscal gauge, demand firm","src":"Reuters via Rallies; Trading Economics; MoF auction result","trend":"down","trendWord":"Just under 4%","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/30-year-bond-yield"},{"change":"−2.8% in Oct","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":50253.91},{"l":"Dec 25","v":50339.48},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53322.85},{"l":"Feb 26","v":58850.27},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51063.72},{"l":"Apr 26","v":59284.92},{"l":"May 26","v":66329.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":70062.32},{"l":"Jul 26","v":64362.02},{"l":"Aug 26","v":66311.93},{"l":"Sep 26","v":66753.72},{"l":"19 Oct","v":64916.7}],"latest":"64,916.70","name":"Nikkei 225","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"66,753.72 on 30 Sep","read":"Closed at 64,916.70 on 19 Oct, down 407.59 points (−0.62%), after falling more than 2% intraday on Friday's US tech selloff and valuation worries ahead of this week's AI results; defensives and value names pared the loss. The index is 11.1% below the June all-time high of 73,007 and 2.8% below the September close, with the 15 Oct close of 64,425.95 as the near low. Nikkei 225 breadth was 111 up, 108 down; Prime breadth 1,413 up, 2,064 down. Taiyo Yuden fell 4.85%, Trend Micro 4.21%, Mercari 3.95% and Lasertec 3.91%; Toho rose 7.04%, SHIFT 5.48%, BayCurrent 5.35% and Inpex 3.71% on oil. After the close the Nasdaq rose 1.11% and the Philadelphia semiconductor index 1.6%, ending an eight-day losing streak, so the 20 Oct session opened with a better lead. March's close of 51,064 marks the Hormuz shock low.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Below 65,000","src":"Nikkei Indexes; News On Japan; Moomoo (Nikkei wire); Trading Economics; Reuters via Rallies","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slipping again","url":"https://www.investing.com/indices/japan-ni225-historical-data"},{"change":"−1.0% in Oct","hist":[{"l":"Dec 24","v":2784.92},{"l":"Mar 25","v":2658.73},{"l":"Jun 25","v":2852.84},{"l":"Sep 25","v":3137.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3408.97},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3497.86},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3994},{"l":"Sep 26","v":4108.65},{"l":"19 Oct","v":4067.48}],"latest":"4,067.48","name":"TOPIX","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"4,108.65 on 30 Sep","read":"Closed at 4,067.48 on 19 Oct, up 1.48 points (+0.04%), holding the 4,050 line while the Nikkei fell 0.62%, so the weakness stays concentrated in AI and semiconductor names and value, energy and defensive names are absorbing it. The broad index is 1.0% below its September close of 4,108.65 and 1.7% above the 15 Oct close of 3,999.34. It meets the TOPIX half of the Neutral test; the Nikkei half (66,000) does not. History shown at quarter-ends only.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Holding above 4,050","src":"investing.com; News On Japan; Moomoo (Nikkei wire); kabutore.biz quarterly closes","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Flat","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/quote/index/TOPIX-INDEX-61714390/"},{"change":"+0.4% (yen weaker)","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":156.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":156.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":154.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":155.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":159.8},{"l":"Apr 26","v":160.2},{"l":"May 26","v":159.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":161.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":160.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":160.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":157.4},{"l":"19 Oct","v":158.05}],"latest":"158.05","name":"USD/JPY","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"157.4 on 30 Sep","read":"Closed at 158.05 on 19 Oct (investing.com) after a 157.70 to 158.13 range, a fifth session inside 157.5 to 158.4; investing.com's equities report showed 157.92. The pair has held 156.50 to 159.00 since 24 Sep; the 14 Oct US CPI spike stopped just under 159.00. Ueda's 19 Oct testimony and Takaichi's line that confidence in the yen comes from strengthening the economy did not move it. The US 10-year near 5.12% with October Fed hold odds about 85% and a December hike about 77%, against a BoJ October hike probability of about 6%, keeps the rate gap wide; Brent at $109.55 widens Japan's import bill and leans against the yen. The MoF spent a record ¥15.4tn (about $96bn) between 30 Jul and 26 Aug after about $73bn in April–May, and 160 remains the intervention trigger.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Near intervention zone","src":"investing.com; FXStreet; babypips; Trading Economics","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Range-bound","url":"https://www.fxstreet.com/news/yen-slips-to-a-three-week-low-on-us-cpi-then-recovers-it-all-202610142330"}],"sub":"Additions to the leading and coincident core. Closing levels for Wednesday 14 Oct unless a date is shown; 15 Oct figures are intraday.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["Leading indicators are unchanged and still positive. The manufacturing PMI is 54.1 and services 51.3, the Tankan put large manufacturers at +24 with a December outlook of +21, August core machinery orders rose 18.6% y/y and machine tool orders 60.4% y/y in September. Consumer confidence at 35.4 is the weak leading reading. The October flash PMIs print on 23 Oct with consensus 54.5 for manufacturing and 51.3 for services.","Coincident data is soft but not breaking. The August tertiary industry index released on 19 Oct fell 0.1% m/m against −0.4% expected after +0.2% in July. August industrial production is +2.7% y/y and −2.4% m/m, real wages rose 1.5% for an eighth straight month, household spending fell 3.1% and the jobs ratio sits at 1.18, below the 1.2 line. September exports print on 21 Oct (August +19.3%, trade balance consensus −¥250bn after −¥1,106bn).","The BoJ stays on a hiking path with October still unlikely. Ueda told parliament on 19 Oct that underlying inflation is 'quite close' to 2%, that strong global AI demand is lifting capex, exports and prices, and that failing to adjust the policy rate appropriately could push up long-term yields; he gave no timing, and Bloomberg's implied probability of an October hike is about 6% with December the expected date for a move to 1.5%. Core CPI is 1.7% only because of subsidies; Tokyo core jumped to 2.7% in September and the national September core CPI on 23 Oct carries a 2.1% consensus (headline 2.3%; Newsquawk sees 2.8% to 3.0%).","Fiscal risk edged up and the market shrugged. Takaichi told parliament on 19 Oct she 'would not rule out the chance of issuing bonds altogether' to fund the two-year food consumption tax cut from April 2027, while promising to control annual issuance appropriately; a Kyodo poll on 18 Oct put cabinet approval at 49.1%, down 4.3 points, after the farm minister's exit. The 10-year JGB still fell to 2.914% on 19 Oct and the 20-year auction on 20 Oct cleared at 3.723% with a record 5.84 bid-to-cover (prior 4.01) and a 0.07 tail, as yields near 30-year highs pull in life insurers.","Markets: 19 Oct closes were Nikkei 64,916.70 (−407.59, −0.62%, after a drop of more than 2% intraday), TOPIX 4,067.48 (+1.48, +0.04%), 10-year JGB 2.914% (−2 bp), 2-year 1.90%, 20-year 3.78%, 30-year 3.99% and USD/JPY 158.05 (range 157.70 to 158.13). Chip names led the losses (Taiyo Yuden −4.85%, Lasertec −3.91%) while Toho rose 7.04% and Inpex 3.71% on oil. After the close the S&P 500 rose 0.41% to 7,690.73 and the Nasdaq 1.11% to 26,430.89 with the Philadelphia semiconductor index +1.6% after an eight-day, 15.7% slide; the US 10-year was about 5.12% and Brent settled at $109.55 (−0.3%) with WTI at $103.66 after Houthi strikes on Saudi energy sites."],"translation":[{"body":"Fundamentals still support the index: Tankan large-manufacturer sentiment +24, capex plans +11.3%, August core machinery orders +18.6% y/y, exports +19.3% with semiconductors +52%, and TSMC's Q4 guidance of US$52.3bn to US$54.3bn says end demand for chips is intact. The problem is concentration, AI financing and oil. The Nikkei fell 0.62% to 64,916.70 on 19 Oct after a drop of more than 2% intraday while TOPIX was flat at 4,067.48, so the damage still sits in AI and semiconductor names that are about 45% of the Nikkei by weight, with Prime breadth 1,413 up and 2,064 down. The Neutral test remains half met (TOPIX above 4,050, Nikkei below 66,000). After the Tokyo close the Nasdaq rose 1.11% and the Philadelphia semiconductor index 1.6% after an eight-day, 15.7% slide, and Brent settled at $109.55, so the 20 Oct lead is better but oil at $110 still hits an energy importer. Under the framework the supportive signals are both PMIs above 50, real wages positive, machinery orders up and unemployment at 2.5%; the opposing ones are confidence at 35.4, industrial production at +2.7% y/y with two monthly falls, household spending −3.1%, a jobs ratio below 1.2, a US rate path still tightening and crude near $110. US AI earnings this week and Japanese earnings from late October decide whether the index or TOPIX has the better read.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"The curve eased again on risk aversion and record auction demand. The 10-year closed at 2.914% on 19 Oct, 15 bp below the 30 Sep close and about 20 bp below the 6 Oct high near 3.12%; the 30-year is 3.99%, 24 bp under the 5 Oct record of 4.235%; the 20-year 3.78% and the 2-year 1.90%, which still prices a December hike. On 20 Oct the ¥700bn 20-year auction cleared at 3.723% with a record 5.84 bid-to-cover (prior 4.01) and a 0.07 tail, after the 10-year drew 3.76, the 30-year 3.88 and the 5-year 3.56 at 2.379% on 14 Oct, so the September-October rise was term premium from Takaichi's food tax cut and heavy issuance rather than a buyers' strike, and yields near 30-year highs are pulling life insurers back into yen debt. Ueda's 19 Oct testimony that under-adjusting the policy rate would push up long-term yields ties the next hike to long-end stability. Takaichi's 19 Oct admission that bonds may fund the tax cut keeps the fiscal premium alive even as the market shrugged. The yen closed at 158.05, a fifth session within 157.5 to 158.4. The US 10-year near 5.12% and a Fed priced at about 77% for a December hike (October hold about 85%) keep the rate gap wide; Brent at $109.55 adds to the import bill. A push toward 160 invites a repeat of the April–May and July–August interventions, while a deeper equity selloff unwinds carry trades and strengthens the yen the other way, as the 15 Oct dip to 157.55 showed.","title":"Fixed income and currency"},{"body":"The BoJ is tightening on two fronts: the policy rate at 1.25% with the next move expected in December (Bloomberg's implied October probability about 6% on 20 Oct; OIS about 88% for December as of 13 Oct), and a balance sheet down to ¥625tn from a ¥765tn peak as JGB purchases fall to ¥2.3tn a month this quarter and ¥2tn from April 2027. Ueda told parliament on 19 Oct that underlying inflation is 'quite close' to 2%, that anchoring it is becoming more important, that AI demand, a tight labour market and Middle East costs are all pushing prices, and that fiscal expansion lifts prices and wages in the short run; emergency JGB purchases are reserved for sharp yield spikes or market dysfunction. Brent at $109.55 after Houthi strikes on Saudi energy sites and Hormuz crude flows of about 8.5 million b/d last week (Kpler) is the upside price risk, since Japan buys about 96% of its crude from the Middle East, and it strengthens the case for extending fuel subsidies. Fiscal policy is expansionary with a ¥21.3tn stimulus last November and the ¥10tn food tax cut from April 2027, which Takaichi said on 19 Oct may be partly bond-funded; the Kyodo poll's 49.1% approval (−4.3 points) after the farm minister's exit weakens her hand before the FY2027 budget. Core CPI at 1.7% is below target only because of subsidies; Tokyo core at 2.7% and core-core at 1.9% say the underlying rate is at or above 2%, and the 23 Oct national core CPI consensus is 2.1%. That print and the 30 Oct Outlook Report decide whether December is a done deal.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
